0ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "q-17151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{15}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{19\\pi }{15}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{26\\pi }{15}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{15}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{15}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "q-17151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta =\\frac{19\\pi }{15}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "q-120708" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/c3d93c8d7d04af6783ef9149e15b5c1a59d1abb2744459004619a5ea2ef029e7.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(4,5)$, find the polar equation of the ray." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "q-120708" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The angles that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the ray lies in the first quadrant, its polar equation is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "q-120708" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8245313d6a1a9363cc6b5889ea8e012c290586419323a4fbba4aa5e1cfd1f2de"} {"question" "q-120708" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "q-120708" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})"] ["math" "\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})"] ["math" "\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{5})"] ["math" "\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{4})"] ["math" "\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{9}{5})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "q-120708" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{4})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "q-120704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/03814c00b9e29f8a05507616ed08e3fe3daa484336e461012dc6c80799efc9e0.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt{6},-\\sqrt{2})$, find the polar equation of the ray." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "q-120704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{-\\sqrt{6}}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the third quadrant. So, we find the angle in the third quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "q-120704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8245313d6a1a9363cc6b5889ea8e012c290586419323a4fbba4aa5e1cfd1f2de"} {"question" "q-120704" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd6