7dd0bf1afc581ce84c60d.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=5$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ intersect at the point $Q,$ as shown above. The Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "q-321918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The polar coordinates of $Q$ are $(r,\\theta )=(5,\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $Q$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.5 \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "q-321918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8245313d6a1a9363cc6b5889ea8e012c290586419323a4fbba4aa5e1cfd1f2de"} {"question" "q-321918" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "q-321918" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "q-321918" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "q-321918" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "q-120730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/84b17449880281367f50da89c39a7bcb1348c3a589aec74d9dec24eadb614ce7.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=8$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "q-120730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(8,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "q-120730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8245313d6a1a9363cc6b5889ea8e012c290586419323a4fbba4aa5e1cfd1f2de"} {"question" "q-120730" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "q-120730" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-2,2)"] ["math" "(-4,4\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "(-4\\sqrt{2},4)"] ["math" "(-4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "(-\\sqrt{8},\\sqrt{8})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "q-120730" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "7573a241f0f104e940ddce4d0579fd4fa7f67cc52e49589d7bd65b1ad94ba99b"} {"question" "e-5389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Plot the graph of the polar curve $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/example-5389.json" "file_sha256" "ad1c0a1002c4997c4c9bc7aeab4540b2b642e73b19319c035786120d2c8a6f8d"} {"question" "e-5389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/8c25245d58ec9ddd8d8f1e73f8eae12c244ecd32018f939f1aba86b274622ba8.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/example-5389.json" "file_sha256" "ad1c0a1002c4997c4c9bc7aeab4540b2b642e73b19319c035786120d2c8a6f8d"} {"question" "e-5390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/245f03c8eeb5b84f7b4caafd61f37b7c15e77e77a447fc762170e05d61c87864.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/example-5390.json" "file_sha256" "e853fdf0962cbab977cdd075a7bd1364617e8bad244b02963678d329cc3d58cd"} {"question" "e-5390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\pi+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/example-5390.json" "file_sha256" "e853fdf0962cbab977cdd075a7bd1364617e8bad244b02963678d329cc3d58cd"} {"question" "e-5391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/1e1362c20f9e20bb22d049d8b218d786a5254603ab4cc36d90b5866095a8897c.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(6,-2\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/example-5391.json" "file_sha256" "87fae17c40c8ed08fe969a3f66277bd4bf600c1dda8c4eee7ab016d9ef1919d8"} {"question" "e-5391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-2\\sqrt{3}}{6}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we find the angle in the fourth quadrant that has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/example-5391.json" "file_sha256" "87fae17c40c8ed08fe969a3f66277bd4bf600c1dda8c4eee7ab016d9ef1919d8"} {"question" "e-5392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The polar curve $r=7$ and the ray $\\theta=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/728f7aad12a4f127cc002bc38c46fa87ba70d6ecd79ef311a0154014b649a0ce.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/example-5392.json" "file_sha256" "38860f750ac39bb6587f1ecf4a081f338313cae6903df2db525a0555f7bd8be9"} {"question" "e-5392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r, \\theta) = \\left(7, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(-\\dfrac 72, -\\dfrac{7\\sqrt 3}2\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/example-5392.json" "file_sha256" "38860f750ac39bb6587f1ecf4a081f338313cae6903df2db525a0555f7bd8be9"}] "usage" {"q-41263" [] "e-5390" [] "e-5391" [] "e-5392" [] "e-5389" []} "basis" 866 "retractions" [[17592186134265 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2173/Source/Images/q-41263.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r = 8$ and the ray $θ = \\frac{5π}{6}$ intersect at the point $P$, as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P$?"] [17592186133480 :question/problem "Plot the graph of the polar curve $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$."] [17592186133480 :question/worked-solution "The polar curve defined by the equation\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nis the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nwith the positive $x$-axis.\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2173/Source/Images/ad92e65ac8f604a06270a7bc13394a1c.png)"] [17592186133481 :question/problem "What is the polar equation of the ray shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2173/Source/Images/5bd17fb1994ce8f71ae772d3e8df931e.png)"] [17592186133481 :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\pi+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$"] [17592186133482 :question/problem "Given that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(6,-2\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2173/Source/Images/24cabf2b7b7d2d79a92e8c18a568d670.png)"] [17592186133482 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= \\arctan \\mid \\frac{y}{x} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\arctan \\mid \\frac{-2\\sqrt{3}}{6} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\arctan (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we find the angle in the fourth quadrant that has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= 2π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 2π - \\frac{π}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{11π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}\n$$"] [17592186133483 :question/problem "The polar curve $r=7$ and the ray $\\theta=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ intersect at the point $P$, as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2173/Source/Images/1987662f932f5b8a82e413e10c2f9101.png)"] [17592186133483 :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n(r, \\theta) = \\left(7, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r\\cos θ \\\\\n&= 7\\cos (\\frac{4π}{3}) \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\\ny &= r\\sin θ \\\\\n&= 7\\sin (\\frac{4π}{3}) \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac 72, -\\dfrac{7\\sqrt 3}2\\right)\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186132033 17592186132034 17592186132035 17592186132036 17592186132037] "immutable_fields" [[17592186132032 :answer-field/correct] [17592186132032 :answer-field/id] [17592186132032 :answer-field/key] [17592186132032 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
